WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:01.677
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
This week, we're

00:00:01.677 --> 00:00:04.390
talking about task queues,
big data, and Stackdriver.

00:00:04.390 --> 00:00:06.490
Welcome to This Week in
Cloud, where we serve you

00:00:06.490 --> 00:00:08.530
the lowest-latency news.

00:00:08.530 --> 00:00:10.090
Just recently
launched for everyone,

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:13.390
Cloud Tasks is a fully managed
service backed by App Engine.

00:00:13.390 --> 00:00:15.580
You can easily create
new queues and offload

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:17.385
any asynchronous
tasks, giving you

00:00:17.385 --> 00:00:19.390
a distributed task queue
that you can integrate

00:00:19.390 --> 00:00:21.010
with any of your apps.

00:00:21.010 --> 00:00:23.170
With built-in scheduling
and de-duplication,

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:25.180
as well as an
easy-to-integrate API,

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:27.730
Cloud Tasks can be a great way
to simplify your architecture

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:29.050
and focus on what matters--

00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:30.827
the users using
your application.

00:00:30.827 --> 00:00:33.160
Check out the details and get
started with a quick start

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:33.730
guide.

00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:36.768
All you need is a GCP
project with App Engine.

00:00:36.768 --> 00:00:39.310
You may already keep up to date
with news about Apache Hadoop

00:00:39.310 --> 00:00:40.030
and Spark.

00:00:40.030 --> 00:00:43.360
But Cloud Dataproc has lots
of new features to try out.

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:45.220
Dataproc is Google's
fully managed Hadoop

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:47.080
and Spark service
and has added tons

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:48.670
of integrations and
features to help

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:50.657
you build big-data solutions.

00:00:50.657 --> 00:00:52.240
This includes support
for new versions

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:54.190
of software like
Ubuntu and Flink,

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:56.650
easy-to-add components, such
as Jupiter and Zeppelin,

00:00:56.650 --> 00:00:59.470
and lots of Google Cloud
integrations, like BigQuery.

00:00:59.470 --> 00:01:01.540
The full details are in
the blog post, as well as

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:04.569
some links for you to try
out Dataproc for yourself.

00:01:04.569 --> 00:01:06.247
But no matter where
your app runs,

00:01:06.247 --> 00:01:08.080
you'll likely need to
know how it's running.

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:10.690
Well, that's one thing
Stackdriver can help with.

00:01:10.690 --> 00:01:13.048
Charles Baer, a Google
Cloud solution architect,

00:01:13.048 --> 00:01:14.590
recently published
a blog post on how

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:16.300
to build your
application and monitor

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:18.928
for long-term metric
analysis, making it easier

00:01:18.928 --> 00:01:21.470
to spot trends that you may not
see from reviewing short time

00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:22.475
frames.

00:01:22.475 --> 00:01:24.100
We've got the high-level
considerations

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:26.380
and an in-depth solution
guide with a reference

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:29.720
implementation, so you can
start to see the big picture.

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:32.290
And if you want to learn a bit
more about Stackdriver itself,

00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:35.308
I definitely recommend checking
out the Stackdriver sandbox.

00:01:35.308 --> 00:01:36.850
It's an open-source
set of tools that

00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:39.868
make it easy to get started and
learn what Stackdriver can do.

00:01:39.868 --> 00:01:41.410
You can clone the
repo and simply run

00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:43.990
a script to set up a full
demo environment, including

00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:46.660
a microservices-based demo
application and traffic

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:48.610
generator, giving you
the benefit of working

00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:50.830
in a real scenario without
disrupting your production

00:01:50.830 --> 00:01:51.913
environment.

00:01:51.913 --> 00:01:53.830
This is a great way to
learn more and practice

00:01:53.830 --> 00:01:56.320
how you might do some SRE
tasks in Google Cloud.

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:58.227
So don't delay, and try it out.

00:01:58.227 --> 00:02:00.310
If you want to read more
about these announcements

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:02.335
or see a full list,
check out the link.

00:02:02.335 --> 00:02:03.460
Have any thoughts or ideas?

00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:05.330
Leave us a comment or tweet us.

00:02:05.330 --> 00:02:07.710
We'll see you in the cloud.

